From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arduino-stc51 maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mcs251: fix ISR, pointer, and overlapping arithmetic lowering

The MCS251 backend can materialize 24-bit data/code pointers through DPXL and
can use DR24/DR28 as untracked address temporaries.  An interrupt generated by
the backend previously saved DPL/DPH but could overwrite those additional
pieces of compiler state while the interrupted code still needed them.

Save DPXL, DR24 and DR28 in every non-naked ISR.  Native PUSH/POP of a double
register transfers four bytes, so account for the full width in the backend's
stack-offset model instead of treating each instruction as a one-byte push.
Advertise the fix through a compiler-owned predefined capability macro so
runtime libraries can retain a source-level fallback without double-saving.

The fixed-register lowering also confused an indirect byte source such as
`@r1` with the direct-memory aliases available only for R0-R7.  When register
allocation selected R8-R15 this emitted `mov ar9,@r1`: the assembler treated
`ar9` as an undefined symbol, while the superficially similar `mov r9,@r1` is
not a legal MCS251 form either.  Lower such loads through the accumulator and
teach the clobber query about that use, producing the legal two-instruction
sequence `mov a,@r1` / `mov r9,a`.  Add a register-pressure regression that
compiles and assembles the previously failing shape.

The pointer-get iCode also carries a literal byte displacement in its right
operand.  Big-endian narrowing uses that displacement to select the low bytes
of a wider object (for example, u16->u8 uses +1 and u32->u24 uses +1), but the
MCS251 pointer lowerings ignored it.  Apply it consistently to direct, near,
paged, far, code, and generic loads.  For 24-bit spaces adjust the full DPX,
and remove the access-only displacement before writing back a separately
fused post-increment.

Finally, byte-at-a-time arithmetic wrote results from low to high without
checking whether a low destination register aliased a high source register
that had not yet been read.  A real allocation used [r7,r6,r5] + 5 as the
source and [r5,r4,r3] as the result, corrupting an object-field address before
a generic byte store.  Detect this destructive overlap, retain each computed
byte on the stack, and write the destination only after all source bytes have
been consumed.

Base: gevico/sdcc-c251 v4.6.0-mcs251-20260804
Commit: b09075b6a93e6afe10645181e3aeff041ea37f87
---
